{
  "@context": "context.jsonld",
  "@id": "storyrunner",
  "@type": "App",
  "position": 17,
  "id": "storyrunner",
  "family": "play",
  "icon": "📖",
  "name": "Finkosphere",
  "surface": "window",
  "url": "../apps/storyrunner/index.html",
  "desc": "A FINK story, sandboxed all the way up",
  "featured": true,
  "capabilities": [
    "story:launch",
    "story:link",
    "story:navigate",
    "story:observe",
    "story:steer",
    "audio",
    "input",
    "vars:read",
    "vars:write"
  ],
  "bus": {
    "publish": [
      "app.storyrunner.*"
    ],
    "subscribe": [
      "wm.mode",
      "audio.volume",
      "ui.skin"
    ]
  },
  "notes": [
    "The narrative runtime, BOXED — a story compiled and played entirely in an opaque-origin frame, reaching the shell only through story:* verbs (spec §5.7, threat model 2026-07-28). It is the only story engine. NO same-origin: it is a real box. story:launch lets a # APP: tag ask the shell to open a (separately boxed) guest — up AND down. FINKOSPHERE — the boxed story engine (owner's name, July 2026). The id stays `storyrunner`: it is in `?app=` links, the snapshot key (`app:storyrunner`), the capUse ledger and the e2e suites. A label is what people read; an id is what things are keyed by.",
    "no `story` override: the runner defaults to its own ./demo.fink.js, resolved relative to the RUNNER frame (a finkapp-relative path would resolve against the wrong base once fetched inside the box) It holds what it CONFERS: a minigame it launches becomes its child, and attenuation requires the child's caps ⊆ the runner's. So the runner carries input + vars:read/write on behalf of the games it instantiates — the capability border is real, not cosmetic. `story:observe` is the LEVEL-1 job from the layer model: this app is the observability point for the story subtree beneath it. It is a separate capability from playing, so a runner that should only play can be handed a list without it and the shell will refuse the verb. `story:steer` it holds only to confer: a world it opens (`# WORLD:`) may ask the story to re-enter the reader's scene (spec §5.8)."
  ]
}
